    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d482">

                <pb facs="0586" n="574" sameAs="frus:frus1940v03/pg574"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">868.24/88: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Greece</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >MacVeagh</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Athens</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-06-21T18:00:00+02:00">June 21, 1940—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 6:37 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>147. The Greek Chief of Staff today urgently requested Captain Johnson<anchor
                        n="60" xml:id="d482-f1"/> to ascertain by telegraph whether the American
                    Army or American industries can supply Greece promptly with 150 French
                    75–millimeter guns, 150 37-to 47-millimeter anti-tank guns, 30 light tanks and 5
                    or 6 anti-aircraft batteries with appurtenances.</p>
                <p>In view of the existing impediments to delivery, the General’s request would
                    appear to merit comment. He maintained that transportation can be found which
                    may of course be possible though it appears to me highly unlikely. But the
                    possibility cannot be wholly excluded that he may be lending himself to some
                    German maneuver to sew up remaining American material in advance of further
                    Allied orders, since German influence here is increasing by leaps and
                    bounds.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">MacVeagh</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d482-f1" n="60" xml:id="d482-f1-content">
                <p>Capt. Max S. Johnson, formerly Assistant Military Attaché in France, on temporary
                    duty in Greece pending arrival of permanent Military Attaché.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
